============================================================
ANTIGRAVITY MASTER VISUAL UI/UX SYSTEM
PREMIUM • ART-DIRECTED • IMAGE-LED • PRODUCTION-GRADE
============================================================

PURPOSE

Build websites that look intentionally designed by a senior
UI/UX designer, visual designer and art director.

The website must NOT look like a generic AI-generated SaaS
template.

The visual design must prioritize:

1. Visual storytelling
2. Strong typography
3. Premium imagery
4. Full-width compositions
5. Intentional whitespace
6. Asymmetric layouts
7. Depth and visual hierarchy
8. Brand identity
9. Content-to-image relationship
10. Responsive composition

Do not optimize for "more UI".

Optimize for:
"better visual composition with less unnecessary UI."

============================================================
1. CORE DESIGN PRINCIPLE
============================================================

DO NOT start by creating cards, containers, buttons and grids.

Start by determining:

- What is the purpose of the section?
- What should the user notice first?
- What is the primary visual story?
- Does the section need imagery?
- Where should the visual focal point be?
- Where should the content-safe negative space be?
- How should the user's eye move through the section?

Then design the UI around that visual composition.

The visual composition comes BEFORE component construction.

============================================================
2. NO GENERIC AI WEBSITE RULE
============================================================

The website must NOT resemble a generic AI-generated website.

AVOID:

- repetitive card grids
- excessive rounded cards
- excessive containers
- excessive borders
- generic gradients
- generic blobs
- generic glassmorphism
- unnecessary shadows
- excessive pills
- repetitive CTA buttons
- identical section layouts
- image-inside-card patterns
- card-inside-card patterns
- excessive dashboard aesthetics
- excessive boxed content
- stock-looking imagery
- repetitive illustrations
- decorative elements without purpose

Never use a card simply because a card is easy to generate.

============================================================
3. BACKGROUND IMAGE RULE
============================================================

Background imagery is a PRIMARY DESIGN ELEMENT.

It is NOT merely decorative.

When a section benefits from visual storytelling, use a
purpose-designed full-width background composition.

Background artwork should:

- extend naturally to the section edges
- have intentional composition
- have foreground/midground/background depth
- contain a clear focal point
- contain appropriate negative space
- support the typography
- support the brand identity
- create atmosphere
- visually communicate the section's purpose

Do NOT:

- put the background image inside a generic card
- put the image inside a rounded image container
- treat the image as a thumbnail
- automatically blur every image
- automatically apply a dark overlay
- use the same background concept repeatedly
- use unrelated stock photography

============================================================
4. IMAGE-FIRST DESIGN PROCESS
============================================================

For every major visual section:

STEP 1:
Define the visual concept.

STEP 2:
Define the focal point.

STEP 3:
Define the content-safe area.

STEP 4:
Define image subject placement.

STEP 5:
Generate/select the visual asset.

STEP 6:
Build the section around the visual.

STEP 7:
Place typography and CTA.

STEP 8:
Evaluate contrast and readability.

STEP 9:
Create mobile-specific composition.

Never design the UI first and simply place an image behind it.

============================================================
5. UNIQUE VISUAL CONCEPT RULE
============================================================

Every major section must have a distinct visual concept.

DO NOT generate the same visual concept repeatedly.

For example:

Hero:
Primary brand story.

Statistics:
Data/analytics visualization.

Technology:
Technology/AI concept.

Security:
Security infrastructure concept.

Performance:
Growth/performance concept.

Global:
Global network/world concept.

CTA:
Premium atmospheric brand concept.

Each section must feel visually related to the brand
but conceptually different.

============================================================
6. BACKGROUND IMAGE COMPOSITION
============================================================

Before using an image, determine content placement.

IF CONTENT IS ON LEFT:

- visual focal point should generally be toward the right
- left side should contain readable negative space
- avoid placing high-detail subjects behind text

IF CONTENT IS ON RIGHT:

- visual focal point should generally be toward the left
- right side should contain readable negative space

IF CONTENT IS CENTER:

- create controlled visual depth
- keep the center readable
- avoid visual clutter behind typography

Never place the primary subject directly behind important text.

============================================================
7. FULL-WIDTH DESIGN RULE
============================================================

Major visual sections should be allowed to use the full viewport.

Do not automatically wrap everything inside:

max-width container
+
white background
+
rounded card.

Use full-width compositions when appropriate.

Content may still use a readable max-width,
but the VISUAL environment should extend beyond it.

============================================================
8. CONTAINER RULE
============================================================

Containers should exist for content readability,
not because every section requires a box.

Use:

- max-width content alignment
- grid systems
- spacing
- typography
- dividers
- whitespace

instead of unnecessary visible containers.

Invisible structure is preferred over visible boxes.

============================================================
9. CARD RULE
============================================================

Cards are OPTIONAL.

Cards should only be used when they provide a clear
functional or information hierarchy.

Good reasons for a card:

- independent interactive item
- pricing plan
- product
- transaction
- selectable option
- important data group
- dashboard module
- comparison item

Bad reasons:

- "because the section needs design"
- "because there are three items"
- "because AI normally creates cards"

If cards are used:

- keep them visually refined
- avoid excessive radius
- avoid excessive shadows
- maintain consistent hierarchy
- do not nest cards
- do not turn every section into a card grid

============================================================
10. SECTION LAYOUT VARIETY
============================================================

Never use the same section composition repeatedly.

Rotate between:

- full-width hero
- editorial layout
- asymmetric split
- image + typography
- immersive background
- overlapping composition
- large statistic
- horizontal feature strip
- visual timeline
- storytelling section
- product showcase
- interactive visualization
- comparison layout
- minimal CTA
- editorial grid
- full-bleed image
- layered composition

The page must have visual rhythm.

============================================================
11. TYPOGRAPHY RULE
============================================================

Typography is a PRIMARY visual element.

Use strong hierarchy:

Display
↓
Headline
↓
Supporting statement
↓
Body
↓
Metadata
↓
CTA

Do not compensate for weak typography by adding cards,
icons or decorative elements.

Use:

- large editorial headlines
- controlled line lengths
- meaningful font weights
- intentional spacing
- strong contrast
- responsive typography

Avoid excessive text inside small UI boxes.

============================================================
12. BUTTON RULE
============================================================

Buttons must NOT dominate the design.

Use clear hierarchy:

PRIMARY CTA
SECONDARY CTA
TEXT/TERTIARY ACTION

Avoid:

- excessive pill-shaped buttons
- multiple primary buttons competing
- gradients unless brand direction requires them
- oversized buttons without purpose
- buttons inside every card

CTA design must reflect the brand.

============================================================
13. ICON RULE
============================================================

Icons should communicate meaning.

Do not add icons merely to make a section look more
"modern".

Avoid:

icon
+
heading
+
paragraph
+
card

repeated across the entire page.

Use icons selectively.

============================================================
14. COLOR RULE
============================================================

Follow the established brand color system.

Do not randomly introduce:

- gradients
- neon colors
- excessive accent colors
- unrelated background colors

Color should establish:

- hierarchy
- emotion
- brand recognition
- interaction states
- visual rhythm

============================================================
15. LIGHT THEME RULE
============================================================

When the project is specified as light theme:

Use a premium light visual language.

Prefer:

- white
- warm/cool neutral surfaces
- subtle tonal separation
- brand accent colors
- high-quality imagery
- soft depth

Do NOT automatically convert sections into dark cards.

Do NOT use dark backgrounds merely to make the website
appear "premium".

============================================================
16. IMAGE QUALITY RULE
============================================================

Images must feel intentionally created for the website.

Prefer:

- cinematic compositions
- realistic lighting
- sophisticated 3D
- premium photography
- abstract visual systems
- editorial imagery
- realistic product visualization
- brand-specific environments

Avoid:

- generic stock images
- obvious AI clichés
- random people looking at laptops
- generic crypto coins
- generic handshake images
- generic office imagery
- unrelated decorative illustrations

============================================================
17. IMAGE GENERATION RULE
============================================================

When a custom background image is required:

GENERATE THE IMAGE SEPARATELY.

The image should contain:

- NO UI
- NO cards
- NO buttons
- NO website text
- NO headings
- NO logo unless explicitly requested
- NO watermark

The image must be designed specifically for the
website section.

The website UI is added AFTER the artwork is approved.

============================================================
18. IMAGE VARIATION RULE
============================================================

Never reuse the same image concept across multiple sections.

Even when the same brand is being represented:

Change:

- composition
- camera angle
- perspective
- subject
- lighting
- depth
- environment
- visual metaphor
- focal point

Maintain brand consistency without visual repetition.

============================================================
19. OVERLAY RULE
============================================================

Do not automatically place a heavy black overlay over
every background image.

Use overlays only when necessary for readability.

Prefer:

- subtle gradients
- controlled tonal transitions
- localized overlays
- content-safe areas
- image composition that already provides contrast

The artwork should remain visible.

============================================================
20. VISUAL DEPTH RULE
============================================================

Whenever appropriate, create depth using:

- foreground elements
- midground elements
- background atmosphere
- lighting
- shadows
- blur
- scale
- perspective
- overlapping elements

Avoid flat compositions.

============================================================
21. SECTION TRANSITION RULE
============================================================

Sections must flow visually into each other.

Avoid:

Section 1 white
↓
Section 2 white
↓
Section 3 white
↓
Section 4 white

Use controlled variation:

- imagery
- tonal backgrounds
- whitespace
- visual separators
- typography
- full-width artwork
- subtle gradients
- overlap

The page should feel like ONE composition.

============================================================
22. RESPONSIVE IMAGE RULE
============================================================

Never blindly use the desktop image on mobile.

For mobile:

- evaluate focal point
- change crop
- reposition content
- change image positioning
- change height
- change overlay
- preserve subject visibility

If necessary, use a mobile-specific image composition.

============================================================
23. MOBILE UI RULE
============================================================

Mobile is NOT a compressed desktop.

Redesign the composition for mobile.

Check:

- typography
- image crop
- content order
- CTA placement
- spacing
- navigation
- touch targets
- section height
- visual hierarchy

Do not simply stack every desktop card vertically.

============================================================
24. MOTION RULE
============================================================

Use subtle premium motion.

Preferred:

- reveal animations
- image parallax
- subtle scale
- opacity transitions
- text reveal
- scroll-linked movement
- hover micro-interactions

Avoid:

- excessive bouncing
- unnecessary spinning
- aggressive animations
- animation on every element
- distracting effects

Motion must communicate hierarchy.

============================================================
25. VISUAL HIERARCHY RULE
============================================================

Every section must have ONE dominant visual priority.

Example:

1. Hero headline
2. Hero visual
3. Supporting content
4. CTA

Do not make:

headline
+
image
+
three cards
+
four icons
+
three buttons
+
statistics

all equally important.

============================================================
26. WHITESPACE RULE
============================================================

Whitespace is an intentional design element.

Do not fill empty space simply because it looks empty.

Empty space can:

- highlight typography
- emphasize imagery
- create premium feeling
- separate concepts
- improve readability

============================================================
27. BRAND STORY RULE
============================================================

Every visual must reinforce the brand.

Ask:

"Could this section belong to any random company?"

If YES:

The visual direction is too generic.

Make it more specific to:

- brand
- industry
- product
- audience
- value proposition
- emotional positioning

============================================================
28. DESIGN CONSISTENCY RULE
============================================================

Consistency does NOT mean every section must look identical.

Maintain consistency through:

- typography
- color system
- spacing system
- interaction language
- border treatment
- imagery style
- motion language

Allow layouts to vary.

============================================================
29. DESIGN CRITIC PASS
============================================================

After completing a page, perform a separate visual critique.

Review as:

Senior UI Designer
+
UX Designer
+
Visual Designer
+
Art Director

Check:

1. Does the website look AI-generated?
2. Are there too many cards?
3. Are there too many containers?
4. Are sections repetitive?
5. Are backgrounds visually meaningful?
6. Are images generic?
7. Is typography strong?
8. Is the visual hierarchy clear?
9. Is the brand recognizable?
10. Does the page have visual rhythm?
11. Are there unnecessary UI elements?
12. Does mobile preserve the visual story?

Then improve the composition.

IMPORTANT:

Do NOT solve weak design by adding more UI.

Prefer removing unnecessary UI and improving composition.

